From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: aiueo52 <aiueo52@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 15:30:56 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 092/105] N1: kernel compiles clean; add a CPU-only Triton
 front-end check

Three GPU-lock windows were spent discovering one Triton compile error at a time, on a
machine where the lock can be held for hours. Two real fixes here:

- FP4_SCALE_BLOCK could not be read from jitted code (Triton 3.7 only allows globals
  instantiated as tl.constexpr), so kernel-side uses now go through _FP4_BLK.
- _load_block_scales branched on a constexpr with an early return in each arm. Triton
  type-checks both arms regardless, so the dead non-native-e4m3 branch's integer
   on an fp8 pointer was a hard error. Rewritten with one assignment and one
  return; the non-native path is deleted (this fork targets sm_120).

bench/n1/compile_check.py runs the Python->TTIR front end for all 36 tile variants
against an explicit sm_120 target with no device, which is where every one of these
surfaced. 36/36 now compile. The smoke test in check_nvfp4_gemv.py also collects
failures across variants instead of raising on the first.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
